  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">Output 1.4. Strengthened social dialogue and labour relations laws, processes and institutions</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="FR">1. A guideline on establishment and functioning of bipartite workplace cooperation committee at the enterprise level has been endorsed by the employers&apos; and workers representatives from the Federation of Nepalese Chamber of Commerce (FNCCI), Nepal Trade Union Congress (NTUC), General Federation of Nepalese Trade Unions (GEFONT) and the All Nepal Trade Union Federation (ANTUF) at a tripartite virtual meeting held on the 7th July 2021. In addition, the employers&apos; and the workers&apos; representatives have agreed to pilot the implementation of workplace cooperation committees in selected enterprises in 2022.
Further, they have agreed to launch an awareness raising campaign on workplace cooperation, with the participation of workplace-level social partners from selected enterprises to share good practice, challenges and opportunities of workplace cooperation mechanisms.

2. A guideline on establishment and functioning of grievance handling mechanism for enterprises prepared, and endorsed on 7th July by the tripartite representatives of the Federation of Nepalese Chamber of Commerce and Industry (FNCCI), Nepal Trade Union Congress (NTUC), General Federation of Nepalese Trade Unions (GEFONT) and the All Nepal Trade Union Federation (ANTUF). The tripartite constituents additionally agreed to pilot these guidelines. The guidelines provide direction to enterprises on the development of enterprise-level grievance handling procedures.

3. Bipartite agreement concluded between Joint Trade Union Coordination Centre (JTUCC), Federation of Nepalese Chambers of Commerce and Industry (FNCCI), and Confederation of Nepalese Industries (CNI) that resolved wage payment and other issues raised during the lockdown in 2020. 1. The ILO provided technical support, ensuring that the guideline is in compliance with ILO Conventions and Recommendations, to draft the guideline and align it with the Labour Act 2017, which provides a legal framework on the formation of labour-relation committee at the enterprise level. The guideline was workshopped with the tripartite constituents and is based on the ILO&apos;s Industrial Relations Toolkit. The ILO facilitated the tripartite consultation meeting and presented the guideline to the tripartite representatives. Further, it conducted an awareness sessions on workplace cooperation with the ILO constituents. Turin Centre also developed a self-learning module on workplace cooperation, based on the ILO&apos;s Industrial Relations Toolkit and customized to Nepali context.       
  		
2. Grievance handling guidelines, prepared by the ILO, were endorsed by the tripartite constituents in July 2021. The guideline was workshopped with the tripartite constituents and is based on the ILO&apos;s Industrial Relations Toolkit. The ILO contribution includes an awareness session on the 7th July 2021 that was conducted on grievance handling, and the technical support that was provided to draft the guidelines. 

The ILO, with the support of the Turin Centre, also developed a distance learning module on grievance handling, based on the ILO&apos;s Industrial Relations Toolkit, specifically customised for Nepal. 

3. ILO facilitated a bipartite dialogue between Joint Trade Union Coordination Centre (JTUCC), Federation of Nepalese Chambers of Commerce and Industry (FNCCI), and Confederation of Nepalese Industries (CNI) to resolve labour relations challenges arising from COVID-19.</narrative>
   </description>
